---
export const title = 'Ranges from the server';
export const order = 200;
import DemoComponentCard from '../../../../components/DemoComponentCard.astro';
---

<p>
  When the search engine already knows where the matches are, for stemmed or fuzzy matches the query's
  words would miss, set the <code>ranges</code> property to <code>[start, end]</code> offsets in the
  element's text. <code>query</code> is then ignored.
</p>

<DemoComponentCard lang={'html'}>
  <wc-highlight id='highlight-ranges'>Running services restart after the nightly update.</wc-highlight>
  <script is:inline>
    // The search engine matched "run" and "restarts" to these words.
    document.querySelector('#highlight-ranges').ranges = [
      [0, 7],
      [17, 24],
    ];
  </script>
</DemoComponentCard>

<h3>Accessibility</h3>
<p>
  Matches are <code>&lt;mark&gt;</code> elements, which some screen readers announce as highlighted.
  Their colour, the tertiary container, differs from links and from the text selection, and in forced
  colours mode they use the system's <code>Mark</code> colour. Use <code>--highlight-background</code>
  and <code>--highlight-color</code> to change them, keeping a 4.5:1 contrast.
</p>
